(17) Tj T*
(Snippet 5: Bad tool documentation) Tj T*
(Describe actions, not implementations) Tj T*
(Assuming each tool is well-documented, the model's instructions should describe actions,) Tj T*
(not specific tools. This is important to eliminate any possibility of conflict between) Tj T*
(instructions on how to use the tool \(which can confuse the LLM\). Where the available tools) Tj T*
(can change dynamically, as with MCP, this is even more relevant.) Tj T*
(* Describe what, not how: Explain what the model needs to do, not how to do it. For) Tj T*
(example, say "create a bug to describe the issue", instead of "use the create_bug tool".) Tj T*
(* Don't duplicate instructions: Don't repeat or re-state the tool instructions or) Tj T*
(documentation. This can confuse the model, and creates an additional dependency) Tj T*
(between the system instructions and the tool implementation.) Tj T*
(* Don't dictate workflows: Describe the objective, and allow scope for the model to use) Tj T*
(tools autonomously, rather than dictating a specific sequence of actions.) Tj T*
(* DO explain tool interactions: If one tool has a side-effect that may affect a different tool,) Tj T*
(document this. For instance, a fetch_web_page tool may store the retrieved web page in) Tj T*
(a file; document this so the agent knows how to access the data.) Tj T*
(Python) Tj T*
(def fetchpd\(pid\):) Tj T*
(""") Tj T*
(Retrieves product data) Tj T*
(Args:) Tj T*
(pid: id) Tj T*
(Returns:) Tj T*
(dict of data) Tj T*
(""") Tj T*
ET
endstream
