(Page 19 of 56 - 03 - Agent Tools &  Interoperability  with MCP) Tj T*
(Agent Tools & Interoperability with MCP) Tj T*
(Updated May 2026) Tj T*
(19) Tj T*
(sequence, defining a single tool to encapsulate many operations may be more efficient. In) Tj T*
(these cases be sure to document very clearly what the tool is doing so the LLM can use) Tj T*
(the tool effectively.) Tj T*
(Design for concise output) Tj T*
(Poorly designed tools can sometimes return large volumes of data, which can adversely) Tj T*
(affect performance and cost.) Tj T*
(* Don't return large responses: Large data tables or dictionaries, downloaded files,) Tj T*
(generated images, etc. can all quickly swamp the output context of an LLM. These) Tj T*
(responses are also frequently stored in an agent's conversation history, so large) Tj T*
(responses can impact subsequent requests as well.) Tj T*
(* Use external systems: Make use of external systems for data storage and access. For) Tj T*
(instance, instead of returning a large query result directly to the LLM, insert it into a) Tj T*
(temporary database table and return the table name, so a subsequent tool can retrieve) Tj T*
(the data directly. Some AI frameworks also provide persistent external storage as part of) Tj T*
(the framework itself, such as the Artifact Service in Google ADK18.) Tj T*
(Use validation effectively) Tj T*
(Most tool calling frameworks include optional schema validation for tool inputs and outputs.) Tj T*
(Use this validation capability wherever possible. Input and output schemas serve two roles) Tj T*
(with LLM tool calling. They serve as further documentation of the tool's capabilities and) Tj T*
(function, giving the LLM a clearer picture of when and how to use the tool; and they provide a) Tj T*
(run-time check on tool operation, allowing the application itself to validate whether the tool is) Tj T*
(being called correctly.) Tj T*
ET
endstream
endobj
