(Page 20 of 56 - 03 - Agent Tools &  Interoperability  with MCP) Tj T*
(Agent Tools & Interoperability with MCP) Tj T*
(Updated May 2026) Tj T*
(20) Tj T*
(Provide descriptive error messages) Tj T*
(Tool error messages are an overlooked opportunity for refining and documenting tool) Tj T*
(capabilities. Often, even well-documented tools will simply return an error code, or at best) Tj T*
(a short, non-descriptive error message. In most tool calling systems, the tool response will) Tj T*
(also be provided to the calling LLM, so it provides another avenue for giving instructions.) Tj T*
(The tool's error message should also give some instruction to the LLM about what to do to) Tj T*
(address the specific error. For example, a tool that retrieves product data could return a) Tj T*
(response that says "No product data found for product ID XXX. Ask the customer to confirm) Tj T*
(the product name, and look up the product ID by name to confirm you have the correct ID.") Tj T*
(Understanding the Model) Tj T*
(Context Protocol) Tj T*
(The "N x M" Integration Problem and the need) Tj T*
(for Standardization) Tj T*
(Tools provide the essential link between an AI agent or an LLM and the external world. The) Tj T*
(ecosystem of externally accessible tools, data sources and other integrations, however,) Tj T*
(is increasingly fragmented and complex. Integrating an LLM with an external tool usually) Tj T*
(requires a custom-built, one-off connector for every pairing of tool and application. This) Tj T*
(leads to an explosion in development effort, often called the "N x M" integration problem,) Tj T*
(where the number of necessary custom connections grows exponentially with each new) Tj T*
(model \(N\) or tool \(M\) added to the ecosystem.19) Tj T*
ET
endstream
endobj
