(Page 23 of 56 - 03 - Agent Tools &  Interoperability  with MCP) Tj T*
(Agent Tools & Interoperability with MCP) Tj T*
(Updated May 2026) Tj T*
(23) Tj T*
(The Communication Layer: JSON-RPC, Transports, and) Tj T*
(Message Types) Tj T*
(All communication between MCP clients and servers is built on a standardized technical) Tj T*
(foundation for consistency and interoperability.) Tj T*
(Base Protocol: MCP uses JSON-RPC 2.0 as its base message format. This gives it a) Tj T*
(lightweight, text-based, and language-agnostic structure for all communications.) Tj T*
(Message Types: The protocol defines four fundamental message types that govern the) Tj T*
(interaction flow:) Tj T*
(* Requests: An RPC call sent from one party to another that expects a response.) Tj T*
(* Results: A message containing the successful outcome of a corresponding request.) Tj T*
(* Errors: A message indicating that a request failed, including code and description.) Tj T*
(* Notifications: A one-way message that does not require a response and cannot be) Tj T*
(replied to.) Tj T*
(Transport Mechanisms: MCP also needs a standard protocol for communication between) Tj T*
(the client and server, called a "transport protocol", to ensure each component is able to) Tj T*
(interpret the other's messages. MCP supports two transport protocols - one for local) Tj T*
(communication and one for remote connections.20) Tj T*
(* stdio \(Standard Input/Output\): Used for fast and direct communication in local) Tj T*
(environments where the MCP server runs as a subprocess of the Host application; used) Tj T*
(when tools need to access local resources such as the user's filesystem.) Tj T*
ET
endstream
endobj
