(Page 25 of 56 - 03 - Agent Tools &  Interoperability  with MCP) Tj T*
(Agent Tools & Interoperability with MCP) Tj T*
(Updated May 2026) Tj T*
(25) Tj T*
(three are offered by the Client to the server. On the server side, these capabilities are: Tools,) Tj T*
(Resources and Prompts; and on the client side, the capabilities are Sampling, Elicitation) Tj T*
(and Roots.) Tj T*
(Of these capabilities defined by the MCP specification, only Tools are broadly supported. As) Tj T*
(the table below shows, while Tools are supported by nearly all tracked client applications,) Tj T*
(Resources and Prompts are only supported by approximately a third, and support for client-) Tj T*
(side capabilities is significantly lower than that. So it remains to be seen whether these) Tj T*
(capabilities will play a significant role in future MCP deployments.) Tj T*
(Table 2: Percentage of publicly available MCP clients supporting MCP server / client capabiliites.) Tj T*
(Source: https://modelcontextprotocol.io/clients , retrieved 15 September 2025) Tj T*
(In this section we will concentrate on Tools, since they have by far the broadest adoption and) Tj T*
(are the core driver of MCP value, and only briefly describe the remaining capabilities.) Tj T*
(Capability) Tj T*
(Client Support Status) Tj T*
(% Supported) Tj T*
(Supported Not supported Unknown/Other) Tj T*
(Tools 78 1 0 99%) Tj T*
(Resources 27 51 1 34%) Tj T*
(Prompts 25 54 0 32%) Tj T*
(Sampling 8 70 1 10%) Tj T*
(Elicitation 3 74 2 4%) Tj T*
(Roots 4 75 0 5%) Tj T*
ET
endstream
