(Page 26 of 56 - 03 - Agent Tools &  Interoperability  with MCP) Tj T*
(Agent Tools & Interoperability with MCP) Tj T*
(Updated May 2026) Tj T*
(26) Tj T*
(Tools) Tj T*
(The Tool22 entity in MCP is a standardized way for a server to describe a function it makes) Tj T*
(available to clients. Some examples might be read_file, get_weather, execute_sql, or) Tj T*
(create_ticket. MCP Servers publish a list of their available tools, including descriptions) Tj T*
(and parameter schemas, for agents to discover.) Tj T*
(Tool Definition) Tj T*
(Tool definitions must conform to a JSON schema23 with the following fields:) Tj T*
(* name: Unique identifier for the tool) Tj T*
(* title: [OPTIONAL] human-readable name for display purposes) Tj T*
(* description: Human- \(and LLM-\) readable description of functionality) Tj T*
(* inputSchema: JSON schema defining expected tool parameters) Tj T*
(* outputSchema: [OPTIONAL]: JSON schema defining output structure) Tj T*
(* annotations: [OPTIONAL]: Properties describing tool behavior) Tj T*
(Tools documentation in MCP should follow the same general best practices we described) Tj T*
(above. For instance, properties such as title and description may be optional in the) Tj T*
(schema, but they should always be included. They provide an important channel for giving) Tj T*
(more detailed instructions to client LLMs about how to use the tool effectively.) Tj T*
ET
endstream
endobj
57 0 obj
<< /Type /Page /Parent 2 0 R /MediaBox [0 0 612 792] /Contents 58 0 R /Resources << /Font << /F1 3 0 R >> >> >>
endobj
58 0 obj
<< /Length 1689 >>
stream
BT
/F1 10 Tf
36 756 Td
13 TL
(Page 27 of 56 - 03 - Agent Tools &  Interoperability  with MCP) Tj T*
(Agent Tools & Interoperability with MCP) Tj T*
(Updated May 2026) Tj T*
(27) Tj T*
(The inputSchema and outputSchema fields are also critical for ensuring correct usage of) Tj T*
(the tool. They should be clearly descriptive and carefully worded, and each property defined) Tj T*
(in both schemas should have a descriptive name and a clear description. Both schema fields) Tj T*
(should be treated as required.) Tj T*
(The annotations field is declared as optional and should remain that way. The properties) Tj T*
(defined in the spec are:) Tj T*
(* destructiveHint: May perform destructive updates \(default: true\).) Tj T*
(* idempotentHint: Calling repeatedly with the same arguments will have no additional) Tj T*
(effect \(default: false\).) Tj T*
(* openWorldHint: May interact with an "open world" of external entities \(default: true\).) Tj T*
(* readOnlyHint: Does not modify its environment \(default: false\)) Tj T*
(* title: Human-readable title for the tool \(note that this is not required to agree with the) Tj T*
(title as provided in the tool definition\).) Tj T*
(All the properties declared in this field are only hints, and are not guaranteed to describe) Tj T*
(the tool's operations accurately. MCP clients should not rely on these properties from) Tj T*
(untrusted servers, and even when the server is trusted, the spec does not require that) Tj T*
(the tool properties are guaranteed to be true. Exercise caution when making use of) Tj T*
(these annotations.) Tj T*
(The following example shows an MCP Tool definition with each of these fields included.) Tj T*
ET
endstream
endobj
