stream
BT
/F1 10 Tf
36 756 Td
13 TL
(Page 29 of 56 - 03 - Agent Tools &  Interoperability  with MCP) Tj T*
(Agent Tools & Interoperability with MCP) Tj T*
(Updated May 2026) Tj T*
(29) Tj T*
(Tool Results) Tj T*
(MCP tools can return their results in a number of ways. Results can be structured or) Tj T*
(unstructured, and can contain multiple different content types. Results can link to other) Tj T*
(resources on the server, and results can also be returned as a single response or a stream) Tj T*
(of responses.) Tj T*
(Unstructured Content) Tj T*
(Unstructured content can take several types. The Text type represents unstructured string) Tj T*
(data; the Audio and Image content types contain base64-encoded image or audio data) Tj T*
(tagged with the appropriate MIME type.) Tj T*
(MCP also allows Tools to return specified Resources, which gives developers more options) Tj T*
(for managing their application workflow. Resources can be returned either as a link to a) Tj T*
(Resource entity stored at another URI, including the title, description, size, and MIME type;) Tj T*
(or fully embedded in the Tool result. In either case, client developers should be very cautious) Tj T*
(about retrieving or using resources returned from an MCP server in this way, and should only) Tj T*
(use Resources from trusted sources.) Tj T*
(Structured Content) Tj T*
(Structured content is always returned as a JSON object. Tool implementers should always) Tj T*
(use the outputSchema capability to provide a JSON schema clients can use to validate) Tj T*
(the tool results, and client developers should validate the tool results against the provided) Tj T*
(schema. Just as with standard function calling, a defined output schema serves a dual) Tj T*
(purpose: it allows the client to interpret and parse the output effectively, and it communicates) Tj T*
(to the calling LLM how and why to use this particular tool.) Tj T*
ET
endstream
endobj
63 0 obj
<< /Type /Page /Parent 2 0 R /MediaBox [0 0 612 792] /Contents 64 0 R /Resources << /Font << /F1 3 0 R >> >> >>
endobj
64 0 obj
<< /Length 1595 >>
stream
BT
/F1 10 Tf
36 756 Td
13 TL
(Page 30 of 56 - 03 - Agent Tools &  Interoperability  with MCP) Tj T*
(Agent Tools & Interoperability with MCP) Tj T*
(Updated May 2026) Tj T*
(30) Tj T*
(Error Handling) Tj T*
(MCP also defines two standard error reporting mechanisms. A Server can return standard) Tj T*
(JSON-RPC errors for protocol issues such as unknown tools, invalid arguments, or server) Tj T*
(errors. It can also return error messages in the tool results by setting the "isError": true) Tj T*
(parameter in the result object. These errors are used for errors generated in the operation) Tj T*
(of the tool itself, such as backend API failures, invalid data, or business logic errors. Error) Tj T*
(messages are an important and often overlooked channel for providing further context to) Tj T*
(the calling LLM. MCP tool developers should consider how best to use this channel for aiding) Tj T*
(their clients in failing over from errors. The following examples show how a developer might) Tj T*
(use each of these error types to provide additional guidance to the client LLM.) Tj T*
(Snippet 7: Example protocol error. Source: https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2025-06-18/server/) Tj T*
(tools#error-handling, retrieved 2025-09-16.) Tj T*
(Python) Tj T*
({) Tj T*
("jsonrpc": "2.0",) Tj T*
("id": 3,) Tj T*
("error": {) Tj T*
("code": -32602,) Tj T*
("message": "Unknown tool: invalid_tool_name. It may be misspelled, or the tool) Tj T*
(may not exist on this server. Check the tool name and if necessary request an) Tj T*
(updated list of tools.") Tj T*
(}) Tj T*
(}) Tj T*
ET
