(32) Tj T*
(Resources) Tj T*
(Resources24 are a server-side capability intended to provide contextual data that can be) Tj T*
(accessed and used by the Host application. Resources provided by an MCP server might) Tj T*
(include the content of a file, a record from a database, a database schema, an image or) Tj T*
(another piece of static data information the server developers intend to be used by a client.) Tj T*
(Commonly cited examples of possible Resources include log files, configuration data, market) Tj T*
(statistics, or structured blobs such as PDFs or images. Introducing arbitrary external content) Tj T*
(into the LLM's context carries significant security risks \(see below\), however, so any resource) Tj T*
(consumed by an LLM client should be validated and retrieved from a trusted URL.) Tj T*
(Prompts) Tj T*
(Prompts25 in MCP are another server-side capability, allowing the server to provide reusable) Tj T*
(prompt examples or templates related to its Tools and Resources. Prompts are intended to) Tj T*
(be retrieved and used by the client to interact directly with an LLM. By providing a Prompt, an) Tj T*
(MCP server can give its clients a higher-level description of how to use the tools it provides.) Tj T*
(While they do have the potential to add value to an AI system, in a distributed enterprise) Tj T*
(environment the use of Prompts introduces some evident security concerns. Allowing a third-) Tj T*
(party service to inject arbitrary instructions into the execution path of the application is risky,) Tj T*
(even when filtered by classifiers, auto-raters, or other LLM-based detection methods, At the) Tj T*
(moment, our recommendation is that Prompts should be used rarely, if at all, until a stronger) Tj T*
(security model is developed.) Tj T*
ET
endstream
endobj
