(Page 34 of 56 - 03 - Agent Tools &  Interoperability  with MCP) Tj T*
(Agent Tools & Interoperability with MCP) Tj T*
(Updated May 2026) Tj T*
(34) Tj T*
(an operation and interact with the human user via the client's UI, allowing the client to) Tj T*
(maintain control of the user interaction and data sharing, while giving the server a way to get) Tj T*
(user input.) Tj T*
(Security and privacy issues are important concerns around this capability. The MCP spec) Tj T*
(notes that "Servers MUST NOT use elicitation to request sensitive information", and that) Tj T*
(users should be clearly informed about the use of the information and able to approve,) Tj T*
(decline or cancel the request. These guidelines are critical to implementing Elicitation in a) Tj T*
(way that respects and preserves user privacy and security. The injunction against requesting) Tj T*
(sensitive information is impossible to enforce in a systematic manner, so client developers) Tj T*
(need to be vigilant about potential misuse of this capability. If a client does not provide) Tj T*
(strong guardrails around elicitation requests and a clear interface for approving or denying) Tj T*
(requests, a malicious server developer could easily extract sensitive information from) Tj T*
(the user.) Tj T*
(Roots) Tj T*
(Roots, the third client-side capability, "define the boundaries of where servers can operate) Tj T*
(within the filesystem"28. A Root definition includes a URI that identifies the root; at the time of) Tj T*
(writing, the MCP specification restricts Root URIs to file: URIs only, but this may change in) Tj T*
(future revisions. A server receiving a Root specification from a client is expected to confine) Tj T*
(its operations just to that scope. In practice, it is not yet clear whether or how Roots would be) Tj T*
(used in a production MCP system. For one thing, there are no guardrails in the specification) Tj T*
(around the behavior of servers with respect to Roots, whether the root is a local file or) Tj T*
(another URI type. The clearest statement about this in the spec is that "servers SHOULD ..) Tj T*
(respect root boundaries during operations."29 Any client developer would be wise not to rely) Tj T*
(too heavily on server behavior regarding Roots.) Tj T*
ET
endstream
endobj
