(Page 35 of 56 - 03 - Agent Tools &  Interoperability  with MCP) Tj T*
(Agent Tools & Interoperability with MCP) Tj T*
(Updated May 2026) Tj T*
(35) Tj T*
(Model Context Protocol: For and Against) Tj T*
(MCP adds several significant new capabilities to the AI developer's toolbox. It also has some) Tj T*
(important limitations and drawbacks, particularly as its usage expands from the locally) Tj T*
(deployed, developer augmentation scenario to remotely deployed, enterprise integration) Tj T*
(applications. In this section we will look first at MCP's advantages and new capabilities; then) Tj T*
(we consider the pitfalls, shortcomings, challenges and risks MCP introduces.) Tj T*
(Capabilities and Strategic Advantages) Tj T*
(Accelerating Development and Fostering a Reusable Ecosystem) Tj T*
(The most immediate benefit of MCP is in simplifying the integration process. MCP provides a) Tj T*
(common protocol for tool integration with LLM-based applications. This should help reduce) Tj T*
(the development cost, and therefore time to market, for new AI-driven features and solutions.) Tj T*
(MCP may also help foster a "plug-and-play" ecosystem where tools become reusable and) Tj T*
(shareable assets. Several public MCP server registries and marketplace have emerged) Tj T*
(already, which allow developers to discover, share, and contribute pre-built connectors. To) Tj T*
(avoid potential fragmentation of the MCP ecosystem, the MCP project recently launched the) Tj T*
(MCP Registry30, which provides both a central source of truth for public MCP servers, and) Tj T*
(also an OpenAPI specification to standardize MCP server declarations. If the MCP registry) Tj T*
(catches on, this may create network effects which could accelerate the growth of the AI) Tj T*
(tool ecosystem.) Tj T*
ET
endstream
endobj
75 0 obj
<< /Type /Page /Parent 2 0 R /MediaBox [0 0 612 792] /Contents 76 0 R /Resources << /Font << /F1 3 0 R >> >> >>
endobj
76 0 obj
<< /Length 1733 >>
stream
BT
/F1 10 Tf
36 756 Td
13 TL
(Page 36 of 56 - 03 - Agent Tools &  Interoperability  with MCP) Tj T*
(Agent Tools & Interoperability with MCP) Tj T*
(Updated May 2026) Tj T*
(36) Tj T*
(Dynamically Enhancing Agent Capabilities and Autonomy) Tj T*
(MCP enhances agent function calling in several important ways.) Tj T*
(* Dynamic Tool Discovery: MCP-enabled applications can discover available tools at) Tj T*
(runtime instead of having those tools hard-coded, allowing for greater adaptability) Tj T*
(and autonomy.) Tj T*
(* Standardizing and Structuring Tool Descriptions: MCP also expands on basic) Tj T*
(LLM function calling by providing a standard framework for tool descriptions and) Tj T*
(interface definitions.) Tj T*
(* Expanding LLM Capabilities: Finally, by enabling the growth of an ecosystem of tool) Tj T*
(providers, MCP dramatically expands the capabilities and information available to LLMs.) Tj T*
(Architectural Flexibility and Future-Proofing) Tj T*
(By standardizing the agent-tool interface, MCP decouples the agent's architecture from) Tj T*
(the implementation of its capabilities. This promotes a modular and composable system) Tj T*
(design, aligning with modern architectural paradigms like the "agentic AI mesh". In such) Tj T*
(an architecture, logic, memory, and tools are treated as independent, interchangeable) Tj T*
(components, making such systems easier to debug, upgrade, scale, and maintain over the) Tj T*
(long term. Such a modular architecture also allows an organization to switch underlying LLM) Tj T*
(providers or replace a backend service without needing to re-architect the entire integration) Tj T*
(layer, provided the new components are exposed via a compliant MCP server.) Tj T*
ET
endstream
endobj
77 0 obj
<< /Type /Page /Parent 2 0 R /MediaBox [0 0 612 792] /Contents 78 0 R /Resources << /Font << /F1 3 0 R >> >> >>
endobj
78 0 obj
<< /Length 1947 >>
stream
BT
/F1 10 Tf
36 756 Td
13 TL
(Page 37 of 56 - 03 - Agent Tools &  Interoperability  with MCP) Tj T*
(Agent Tools & Interoperability with MCP) Tj T*
(Updated May 2026) Tj T*
(37) Tj T*
(Foundations for Governance and Control) Tj T*
(While MCP's native security features are currently limited \(as detailed in the next section\),) Tj T*
(its architecture does at least provide the necessary hooks for implementing more robust) Tj T*
(governance. For instance, security policies and access controls can be embedded within) Tj T*
(the MCP server, creating a single point of enforcement that ensures any connecting agent) Tj T*
(adheres to predefined rules. This allows an organization to control what data and actions are) Tj T*
(exposed to its AI agents.) Tj T*
(The Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform provides native infrastructure to enforce this) Tj T*
(governance. Central to this is the Agent Registry, a unified catalog for discovering and) Tj T*
(managing approved tools, MCP servers, and agent configurations across the enterprise.) Tj T*
(Pairing with this is Agent Identity, a system that assigns a unique, cryptographic ID to every) Tj T*
(individual agent. This guarantees complete traceability, verifiable auditing, and the ability to) Tj T*
(apply fine-grained access controls at the agent level, ensuring that any action taken via MCP) Tj T*
(is fully authorized.) Tj T*
(Furthermore, the protocol specification itself establishes a philosophical foundation for) Tj T*
(responsible AI by explicitly recommending user consent and control. The specification) Tj T*
(mandates that hosts should obtain explicit user approval before invoking any tool or sharing) Tj T*
(private data. This design principle promotes the implementation of "human-in-the-loop") Tj T*
(workflows, where the agent can propose an action but must await human authorization) Tj T*
(before execution, providing a critical safety layer for autonomous systems.) Tj T*
ET
endstream
endobj
79 0 obj
<< /Type /Page /Parent 2 0 R /MediaBox [0 0 612 792] /Contents 80 0 R /Resources << /Font << /F1 3 0 R >> >> >>
endobj
80 0 obj
<< /Length 1824 >>
stream
BT
/F1 10 Tf
36 756 Td
13 TL
(Page 38 of 56 - 03 - Agent Tools &  Interoperability  with MCP) Tj T*
(Agent Tools & Interoperability with MCP) Tj T*
(Updated May 2026) Tj T*
(38) Tj T*
(Critical Risks and Challenges) Tj T*
(A key focus for enterprise developers adopting MCP is the need to layer in support for) Tj T*
(enterprise-level security requirements \(authentication, authorization, user isolation,) Tj T*
(etc.\). Security is such a critical topic for MCP that we dedicate a separate section of this) Tj T*
(whitepaper to it \(see Section 5\). In the remainder of this section, we will look at other) Tj T*
(considerations for deploying MCP in enterprise applications.) Tj T*
(Performance and Scalability Bottlenecks) Tj T*
(Beyond security, MCP's current design presents fundamental challenges to performance and) Tj T*
(scalability, primarily related to how it manages context and state.) Tj T*
(* Context Window Bloat: For an LLM to know which tools are available, the definitions and) Tj T*
(parameter schemas for every tool from every connected MCP server must be included) Tj T*
(in the model's context window. This metadata can consume a significant portion of the) Tj T*
(available token, resulting in increased cost and latency, and causing the loss of other) Tj T*
(critical context information.) Tj T*
(* Degraded Reasoning Quality: An overloaded context window can also degrade the) Tj T*
(quality of the AI's reasoning. With many tool definitions in a prompt, the model may) Tj T*
(have difficulty identifying the most relevant tool for a given task or may lose track of) Tj T*
(the user's original intent. This can lead to erratic behavior, such as ignoring a useful tool) Tj T*
(or invoking an irrelevant one, or ignoring other important information contained in the) Tj T*
(request context.) Tj T*
ET
endstream
endobj
81 0 obj
<< /Type /Page /Parent 2 0 R /MediaBox [0 0 612 792] /Contents 82 0 R /Resources << /Font << /F1 3 0 R >> >> >>
endobj
82 0 obj
<< /Length 2408 >>
stream
BT
/F1 10 Tf
36 756 Td
13 TL
(Page 39 of 56 - 03 - Agent Tools &  Interoperability  with MCP) Tj T*
(Agent Tools & Interoperability with MCP) Tj T*
(Updated May 2026) Tj T*
(39) Tj T*
(* Stateful Protocol Challenges: Using stateful, persistent connections for remote) Tj T*
(servers can lead to more complex architectures that are harder to develop and maintain.) Tj T*
(Integrating these stateful connections with predominantly stateless REST APIs often) Tj T*
(requires developers to build and manage complex state-management layers, which can) Tj T*
(hinder horizontal scaling and load balancing.) Tj T*
(The issue of context window bloat represents an emerging architectural challenge -- the) Tj T*
(current paradigm of pre-loading all tool definitions into the prompt is simple but does not) Tj T*
(scale. This reality may force a shift in how agents discover and utilize tools. One potential) Tj T*
(future architecture might involve a RAG-like approach for tool discovery itself.31 An agent,) Tj T*
(when faced with a task, would first perform a "tool retrieval" step against a massive, indexed) Tj T*
(library of all possible tools to find the few most relevant ones. Based on that response, it) Tj T*
(would load the definitions for that small subset of tools into its context window for execution.) Tj T*
(This would transform tool discovery from a static, brute-force loading process into a) Tj T*
(dynamic, intelligent, and scalable search problem, creating a new and necessary layer in the) Tj T*
(agentic AI stack. Dynamic tool retrieval does, however, open another potential attack vector;) Tj T*
(if an attacker gains access to the retrieval index, he or she could inject a malicious tool) Tj T*
(schema into the index and trick the LLM into calling an unauthorized tool.) Tj T*
(Enterprise Readiness Gaps) Tj T*
(While MCP is rapidly being adopted, several critical enterprise-grade features are still) Tj T*
(evolving or not yet included in the core protocol, creating gaps that organizations must) Tj T*
(address themselves.) Tj T*
(* Authentication and Authorization: The initial MCP specification did not originally) Tj T*
(include a robust, enterprise-ready standard for authentication and authorization. While) Tj T*
(the specification is actively evolving, the current OAuth implementation has been noted to) Tj T*
(conflict with some modern enterprise security practices32.) Tj T*
ET
endstream
endobj
83 0 obj
<< /Type /Page /Parent 2 0 R /MediaBox [0 0 612 792] /Contents 84 0 R /Resources << /Font << /F1 3 0 R >> >> >>
endobj
84 0 obj
<< /Length 2159 >>
stream
BT
/F1 10 Tf
36 756 Td
13 TL
(Page 40 of 56 - 03 - Agent Tools &  Interoperability  with MCP) Tj T*
(Agent Tools & Interoperability with MCP) Tj T*
(Updated May 2026) Tj T*
(40) Tj T*
(* Identity Management Ambiguity: The base MCP protocol does not natively standardize) Tj T*
(identity propagation, making it ambiguous whether an action is initiated by a user, an) Tj T*
(agent, or a system account. The Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform resolves this natively) Tj T*
(through Agent Identity, which assigns cryptographic, verifiable identities to agents,) Tj T*
(enabling strict accountability and fine-grained access control.) Tj T*
(* Lack of Native Observability: The base protocol does not define standards for) Tj T*
(observability primitives like logging, tracing, and metrics, essential capabilities for) Tj T*
(debugging, health monitoring and threat detection. To address this, enterprise) Tj T*
(software providers are building features on top of MCP with offerings like the Apigee) Tj T*
(API management platform, which adds a layer of observability and governance to MCP) Tj T*
(traffic. While MCP lacks built-in observability primitives, to help enterprise platforms) Tj T*
(fill this void,  Google Cloud's new Agent Observability suite provides OTel-compliant) Tj T*
(telemetry, detailed execution traces, and logging. Furthermore, Agent Simulation enables) Tj T*
(developers to stress-test agents at scale against synthetic traffic, ensuring reliability) Tj T*
(before deploying to production.) Tj T*
(MCP was designed for open, decentralized innovation, which spurred its rapid growth, and) Tj T*
(in the local deployment scenario, this approach is successful. However, the most significant) Tj T*
(risks it presents--supply chain vulnerabilities, inconsistent security, data leakage, and a) Tj T*
(lack of observability--are all consequences of this decentralized model. As a result, major) Tj T*
(enterprise players are not adopting the "pure" protocol but are instead wrapping it in layers) Tj T*
(of centralized governance. These managed platforms impose the security, identity, and) Tj T*
(control that extend the base protocol.) Tj T*
ET
endstream
endobj
