stream
BT
/F1 10 Tf
36 756 Td
13 TL
(Page 41 of 56 - 03 - Agent Tools &  Interoperability  with MCP) Tj T*
(Agent Tools & Interoperability with MCP) Tj T*
(Updated May 2026) Tj T*
(41) Tj T*
(Security in MCP) Tj T*
(New threat landscape) Tj T*
(Along with the new capabilities MCP offers by connecting agents to tools and resources) Tj T*
(comes a new set of security challenges that go beyond traditional application) Tj T*
(vulnerabilities.33 The risks introduced by MCP result from two parallel considerations: MCP as) Tj T*
(a new API surface, and MCP as a standard protocol.) Tj T*
(As a new API surface, the base MCP protocol does not inherently include many of the) Tj T*
(security features and controls implemented in traditional API endpoints and other systems.) Tj T*
(Exposing existing APIs or backend systems via MCP may lead to new vulnerabilities if the) Tj T*
(MCP service does not implement robust capabilities for authentication / authorization, rate) Tj T*
(limiting and observability.) Tj T*
(As a standard agent protocol, MCP is being used for a broad range of applications,) Tj T*
(including many involving sensitive personal or enterprise information as well as applications) Tj T*
(in which the agent interfaces with a backend system to take some real-world action. This) Tj T*
(broad applicability increases the likelihood and potential severity of security issues, most) Tj T*
(prominently unauthorized actions and data exfiltration.) Tj T*
(As a result, securing MCP requires a proactive, evolving, and multi-layered approach that) Tj T*
(addresses both new and traditional attack vectors.) Tj T*
ET
endstream
endobj
87 0 obj
<< /Type /Page /Parent 2 0 R /MediaBox [0 0 612 792] /Contents 88 0 R /Resources << /Font << /F1 3 0 R >> >> >>
endobj
88 0 obj
<< /Length 1988 >>
stream
BT
/F1 10 Tf
36 756 Td
13 TL
(Page 42 of 56 - 03 - Agent Tools &  Interoperability  with MCP) Tj T*
(Agent Tools & Interoperability with MCP) Tj T*
(Updated May 2026) Tj T*
(42) Tj T*
(Risks and Mitigations) Tj T*
(Among the broader landscape of MCP security threats, several key risks stand out as) Tj T*
(particularly prominent and worth identifying.) Tj T*
(Top Risks & Mitigations) Tj T*
(Dynamic Capability Injection) Tj T*
(Risk) Tj T*
(MCP servers may dynamically change the set of tools, resources, or prompts they offer) Tj T*
(without explicit client notification or approval. This can potentially allow agents to) Tj T*
(unexpectedly inherit dangerous capabilities or unapproved / unauthorized tools.) Tj T*
(While traditional APIs are also subject to on-the-fly updates that can alter functionality, MCP) Tj T*
(capabilities are much more dynamic. MCP Tools are designed to be loaded at runtime by any) Tj T*
(new agent connecting to the server, and the list of tools itself is intended to be dynamically) Tj T*
(retrieved via a tools/list request. MCP Servers are also not required to notify clients when) Tj T*
(their list of published tools changes. Combined with other risks or vulnerabilities, this could) Tj T*
(be exploited by a malicious server to cause unauthorized behavior in the client.) Tj T*
(More specifically, dynamic capability injection can extend an agent's capabilities beyond) Tj T*
(its intended domain and corresponding risk profile. For example, a poetry-authoring agent) Tj T*
(may connect to a Books MCP server, a content retrieval and search service, to fetch quotes,) Tj T*
(a low-risk, content generation activity. However, suppose the Books MCP service suddenly) Tj T*
(adds a book purchasing capability, in a well-intentioned attempt to provide more value to its) Tj T*
(users. Then this formerly low-risk agent could suddenly gain the ability to purchase books) Tj T*
(and initiate financial transactions, a much higher risk activity.) Tj T*
ET
endstream
endobj
89 0 obj
<< /Type /Page /Parent 2 0 R /MediaBox [0 0 612 792] /Contents 90 0 R /Resources << /Font << /F1 3 0 R >> >> >>
endobj
90 0 obj
<< /Length 2511 >>
stream
BT
/F1 10 Tf
36 756 Td
13 TL
(Page 43 of 56 - 03 - Agent Tools &  Interoperability  with MCP) Tj T*
(Agent Tools & Interoperability with MCP) Tj T*
(Updated May 2026) Tj T*
(43) Tj T*
(Mitigations) Tj T*
(* Explicit allowlist of MCP tools: Implement client-side controls within the SDK or the) Tj T*
(containing application to enforce an explicit allowlist of permitted MCP tools and servers.) Tj T*
(* Mandatory Change Notification: Require that all changes to  MCP server manifests) Tj T*
(MUST set the listChanged flag and allow clients to revalidate server definitions.) Tj T*
(* Tool and Package Pinning: For installed servers, pin the tool definitions to a specific) Tj T*
(version or hash. If a server dynamically changes a tool's description or API signature after) Tj T*
(the initial vetting, the Client must alert the user or disconnect immediately.) Tj T*
(* Secure API / Agent Gateway: API Gateways such as Google's Apigee already provide) Tj T*
(similar capabilities for standard APIs. Increasingly, these products are being augmented) Tj T*
(to provide this functionality for Agentic AI applications and MCP servers. For example,) Tj T*
(Apigee can inspect the MCP server's response payload and apply a user-defined policy) Tj T*
(to filter the list of tools, ensuring the client only receives tools that are centrally approved) Tj T*
(and on the enterprise's allowlist. It can also apply user-specific authorization controls on) Tj T*
(the list of tools that is returned.) Tj T*
(* Furthermore acting as the 'air traffic control' for the agent ecosystem, an Agent) Tj T*
(Gateway intercepts all client-to-agent and agent-to-anywhere traffic. Google Cloud's) Tj T*
(Agent Gateway provides secure, unified connectivity to MCP servers and external tools.) Tj T*
(It centrally enforces access control policies, filters the list of available tools to prevent) Tj T*
(dynamic capability injection, and natively integrates with Model Armor to actively inspect) Tj T*
(payloads, safeguarding the system against prompt injection and sensitive data leakage.) Tj T*
(* Host MCP servers in a controlled environment: Dynamic capability injection is a risk) Tj T*
(whenever the MCP server can change without the knowledge or authorization of the agent) Tj T*
(developer. This can be mitigated by ensuring that the server is also deployed by the agent) Tj T*
(developer in a controlled environment, either in the same environment as the agent or in a) Tj T*
(remote container managed by the developer.) Tj T*
ET
endstream
endobj
