100 0 obj
<< /Length 2177 >>
stream
BT
/F1 10 Tf
36 756 Td
13 TL
(Page 48 of 56 - 03 - Agent Tools &  Interoperability  with MCP) Tj T*
(Agent Tools & Interoperability with MCP) Tj T*
(Updated May 2026) Tj T*
(48) Tj T*
(* Taint Sources/Sinks: In particular, both inputs and outputs should be tagged as "tainted") Tj T*
(or "not tainted". Specific input fields that should be considered "tainted" by default) Tj T*
(include user-provided free-text, or data fetched from an external, less trusted system.) Tj T*
(Outputs that may be generated from tainted data or may be affected by tainted data) Tj T*
(should also be considered tainted. This might include specific fields within outputs, or) Tj T*
(operations such as "send_email_to_external_address", or "write_to_public_database".) Tj T*
(No support for limiting the scope of access) Tj T*
(Risk) Tj T*
(The MCP protocol only supports coarse-grained client-server authorization39. In the MCP) Tj T*
(auth protocol, a client registers with a server in a one-time authorization flow. There is no) Tj T*
(support for further authorization on a per-tool or per-resource basis, or for natively passing) Tj T*
(on the client credentials to authorize access to the resources exposed by the tools. In an) Tj T*
(agentic or multi-agentic system this is particularly important, since the capabilities of the) Tj T*
(agent to act on behalf of the user should be restricted by the credentials the user offers.) Tj T*
(Mitigations) Tj T*
(* Tool invocation should use audience and Scoped credentials: The MCP server must) Tj T*
(rigorously validate that the token it receives is intended for its use \(audience\) and that the) Tj T*
(requested action is within the token's defined permissions \(scope\). Credentials should be) Tj T*
(scoped, bound to authorized callers, and have short expiration periods.) Tj T*
(* Use principle of least privilege: If a tool only needs to read a financial report, it should) Tj T*
(have "read-only" access, not "read-write" or "delete" permissions. Avoid using a single,) Tj T*
(broad credential for multiple systems, and carefully audit permissions granted to agent) Tj T*
(credentials to ensure there are no excess privileges.) Tj T*
ET
endstream
endobj
101 0 obj
<< /Type /Page /Parent 2 0 R /MediaBox [0 0 612 792] /Contents 102 0 R /Resources << /Font << /F1 3 0 R >> >> >>
endobj
102 0 obj
<< /Length 2195 >>
stream
BT
/F1 10 Tf
36 756 Td
13 TL
(Page 49 of 56 - 03 - Agent Tools &  Interoperability  with MCP) Tj T*
(Agent Tools & Interoperability with MCP) Tj T*
(Updated May 2026) Tj T*
(49) Tj T*
(* Secrets and credentials should be kept out of the agent context: Tokens, keys, and) Tj T*
(other sensitive data used to invoke tools or access backend systems should be contained) Tj T*
(within the MCP client and transmitted to the server through a side channel, not through) Tj T*
(the agent conversation. Sensitive data must not leak back into the agent's context, e.g.) Tj T*
(through inclusion in the user conversation \("please enter your private key"\).) Tj T*
(Conclusion) Tj T*
(Foundation models, when isolated, are limited to pattern prediction based on their training) Tj T*
(data. On their own, they cannot perceive new information or act upon the external world;) Tj T*
(tools give them these capabilities. As this paper has detailed, the effectiveness of these) Tj T*
(tools depends heavily on deliberate design. Clear documentation is crucial, as it directly) Tj T*
(instructs the model . Tools must be designed to represent granular, user-facing tasks, not just) Tj T*
(mirror complex internal APIs . Furthermore, providing concise outputs and descriptive error) Tj T*
(messages  is essential for guiding an agent's reasoning. These design best practices  form) Tj T*
(the necessary foundation for any reliable and effective agentic system.) Tj T*
(The Model Context Protocol \(MCP\) was introduced as an open standard to manage this) Tj T*
(tool interaction, aiming to solve the "N x M" integration problem and foster a reusable) Tj T*
(ecosystem. While its ability to dynamically discover tools provides an architectural basis) Tj T*
(for more autonomous AI , this potential is accompanied by substantial risks for enterprise) Tj T*
(adoption. MCP's decentralized, developer-focused origins mean it does not currently include) Tj T*
(enterprise-grade features for security, identity management, and observability. This gap) Tj T*
(creates a new threat landscape, including attacks like Dynamic Capability Injection , Tool) Tj T*
(Shadowing , and "confused deputy" vulnerabilities.) Tj T*
ET
endstream
endobj
