stream
BT
/F1 10 Tf
36 756 Td
13 TL
(Page 50 of 56 - 03 - Agent Tools &  Interoperability  with MCP) Tj T*
(Agent Tools & Interoperability with MCP) Tj T*
(Updated May 2026) Tj T*
(50) Tj T*
(The future of MCP in the enterprise, therefore, will likely not be its "pure" open-protocol) Tj T*
(form but rather a version integrated with layers of centralized governance and control. This) Tj T*
(creates an opportunity for platforms that can enforce the security and identity policies not) Tj T*
(natively present in MCP. Adopters must implement a multi-layered defense, leveraging API) Tj T*
(gateways for policy enforcement , mandating hardened SDKs with explicit allowlists, and) Tj T*
(adhering to secure tool design practices. MCP provides the standard for tool interoperability,) Tj T*
(but the enterprise bears the responsibility of building the secure, auditable, and reliable) Tj T*
(framework required for its operation.) Tj T*
(Appendix) Tj T*
(Confused Deputy problem) Tj T*
(The "confused deputy" problem is a classic security vulnerability where a program with) Tj T*
(privileges \(the "deputy"\) is tricked by another entity with fewer privileges into misusing its) Tj T*
(authority, performing an action on behalf of the attacker.) Tj T*
(With Model Context Protocol \(MCP\), this problem is particularly relevant because the MCP) Tj T*
(server itself is designed to act as a privileged intermediary, with access to critical enterprise) Tj T*
(systems. An AI model, which a user interacts with, can become the "confused" party that) Tj T*
(issues the instructions to the deputy \(the MCP server\).) Tj T*
(Here's a real-world example:) Tj T*
ET
endstream
endobj
